Cell[TextData[{
 "Each node in this graph represents the sector of the Feynman integral \
corresponding to the ",
 "integration variables that appear above it (for instance, the top-left \
node, labeled by ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"{", 
   RowBox[{"x1", ",", "x3"}], "}"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontFamily->"Source Sans Pro",ExpressionUUID->
  "6cedc4be-c424-4aa6-9d9d-b095cf35c84f"],
 ", corresponds to the sector defined by ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{"x2", "=", "x4", "=", "0"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontFamily->"Source Sans Pro",ExpressionUUID->
  "8eecb1c5-f6bd-42e3-a1a3-977b480f9adf"],
 "); the indices of the singularities that appear in each of these sectors \
are displayed below ",
 "the node. Allowed sequences of discontinuities can be read off from this \
graph by either ",
 "choosing a singularity at the current node, or one that can be reached by \
following any ",
 "sequence of directed arrows. If the option ",
 Cell[BoxData["\"\<RefineIndeterminates\>\""], "InlineFormula",
  FontFamily->"Source Sans Pro",ExpressionUUID->
  "675202f3-f4d4-4035-a3ea-b9807797e116"],
 " is enabled, some singularities may be placed on edges (marked in orange). \
These represent ",
 "non-repeating discontinuities."
}], "TechNoteText",
 CellID->591733012,ExpressionUUID->"dc0d9976-5f59-461d-8d6f-dcfed1979796"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

